  "author": "laozi",
  "attributionNote": "The sayings are from the Daodejing, traditionally ascribed to Laozi. Lionel Giles arranges extracts from the eighty-one chapters under nine themes rather than in chapter order, so the book is a selection and not a translation of the whole text.",
  "description": "Lionel Giles's small anthology takes the Daodejing apart and reassembles it by subject: Tao in its transcendental aspect, Tao as a moral principle, the doctrine of inaction, lowliness and humility, government, war, paradoxes, miscellaneous precepts, and Lao Tzŭ on himself. The effect is a portrait of the teaching rather than of the book, and it was for many readers in the first half of the twentieth century the way into Laozi's thought.",
  "historicalContext": "Giles, keeper of the Department of Oriental Printed Books and Manuscripts at the British Museum, wrote this volume for the Wisdom of the East series, which introduced Asian classics to a popular British readership before the First World War. It appeared in 1905, the same year as his book on Sun Tzŭ, and reflects the era's interest in Daoism as a philosophy of quietism rather than a religion.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete. All nine thematic chapters are streamed from the Internet Sacred Text Archive at read time. The series' editorial note and Giles's introduction are not part of the work.",
